  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 355 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nModel consensus remains fair about a potential low developing along \na stalled frontal boundary around C Fl, which then slowly migrates \nnorthward across the W Atlantic. GFS is the more aggressive solution \nshowing a stronger low on Wednesday, but regardless of the final \noutcome of this feature, guidance agreement depicts an overall wet \npattern persisting through the end of the work week. This will also \nresult in having a rather weak southerly flow (or even clam at \ntimes), which may combine with the influence of the low to enhance \ntropical moisture advection across the state. NBM shows Wednesday \nand Thursday as the wettest days with highest POPs in the 80-90% \nrange for much of the area.  \n\nThe overall light flow regime will also allow for afternoon sea \nbreeze and storm outflow boundaries to drive storm development and \nmotion. Main hazards will include damaging gusts, lightning strikes, \nand localized flooding. WPC day-3 outlook includes Palm Beach county \nand portions of the Lake region in their excessive rainfall outlook \nmarginal category. Therefore, expect a rather rainy weather pattern \nto prevail through Friday.  \n\nHigh pressure should then reestablish in the wake of the low/front \nfor the start of the weekend, bringing back a more typical easterly \nwind regime, along with decreasing POPs/Wx coverage. \n\nLatest NBM temps show a modest cooling trend for Wednesday and \nThursday with afternoon highs remaining in the mid-upper 80s, then \nback to around 90 or low 90s for the rest of the upcoming weekend.",
